  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1054 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAnother chilly night will unfold with mostly clear skies and\nlight to calm winds. Expect patchy fog, some locally dense, to\ndevelop once more as dewpoint depressions sink. Any fog should\ndiminish shortly after sunrise and a slightly above normal\ntemperature fall day will unfold. The warming will continue as\nthe synoptic pattern begins to change with troughing emerging in\nthe west. Moisture will pull ahead of the trough, supplying\nmodest warming as well as increasing dewpoint values. This trough\nis on the weaker side so minimal impacts are expected. The best\nPW values creep up to around the 75th percentile on Wednesday.\nGiven this prolonged strong area of high pressure, it will take\nmuch more moisture than currently projected to bring high QPF\nvalues across the region. Current probabilities from the LREF for\nwetting rainfall (0.10\" ) or more from Wednesday morning through\nThursday morning are hovering around 60% near the Tennessee River\nValley with lesser values elsewhere. These probabilities drop off\nsignificantly (25% or less) for receiving 0.25\" of rainfall or\nmore.\n\nAn attendant cold front will also sweep the region Thursday\nassociated with a low pressure system from the parent trough,\nbringing continental polar air behind it. Temperatures will begin\nto plummet behind this front, with Thursday's forecast highs in\nthe upper 50s to lower 60s.. Though we are still several days\nout, Friday looks to be pretty brisk as the pressure gradient\nbehind this deepening trough begins to tighten. Saturday morning\ncould also bring our first areawide freeze of the season with\nprobabilities from the NBM for temperatures <32F are greater than\n50%.\n\nDNM",
